;; ============================================================================

;; CONTROL FLOW GRAPH NODES  —  port of Simple's `node/CFGNode.java`
;; ============================================================================
;;
;; ## What a CFG node is, in a sea of nodes
;;
;; There are no basic blocks. The graph is a soup of data nodes with no location, held together by a
;; CONTROL SUBGRAPH threaded through it: Start, Region, Loop, If, CProj, Return, Stop. Those are the
;; CFG nodes. Every question the backend asks — where may this node execute, which loop is it in,
;; how hot is it — is a question about that subgraph, and blocks only exist once Global Code Motion
;; invents them.
;;
;; Simple's taxonomy, reproduced because the block-head / block-tail split drives GCM and encoding:
;;
;;     Start   - Block head; has constants and trailing Funs
;;     Region  - Block head; has Phis.  Includes Fun and Parms.
;;     CallEnd - Block head; followed by CProj and Proj; also linked RPC Parms
;;     CProj   - Block head
;;
;;     If      - Block tail; followed by CProj same block
;;     Call    - Block tail; followed by CallEnd and linked Funs
;;     New     - If followed by CProj: block tail, else mid-block; followed by Proj
;;
;; ## idepth: lazy, approximate, and valid mid-parse
;;
;; Simple computes the immediate-dominator DEPTH lazily on first request, and it is valid DURING
;; PARSING, before the whole program exists. That matters: peepholes ask dominance questions while
;; the graph is still being built, and a pass demanding a complete program could not run there.
;;
;; It is an APPROXIMATION of a real dominator computation, which is the right trade when the graph
;; is being rewritten under you constantly.
;;
;; ## idom is per-kind, and Region's is the interesting one
;;
;; For most CFG nodes the immediate dominator is `in(0)` — the single control predecessor. A Region
;; has many, so its idom is the LCA of all of them, and its idepth is the MAX over its inputs rather
;; than one-plus-parent. A Loop bypasses that entirely: its idom is its ENTRY, because the back edge
;; does not dominate anything. Start has no dominator at all.
;;
;; `idom-lca` walks the two dominator chains upward in parallel, stepping whichever side is deeper,
;; until they meet. Simple notes it does NOT cache and so can be linear in the program size —
;; accepted deliberately, because caching a dominator through a graph that peepholes are rewriting
;; is how you get a stale answer that schedules a node outside its own dominance region.
;;
;; ## Staleness is version-checked
;;
;; The cache is guarded by a global control version (`invalidate-cfg!` in aot.node.node), bumped
;; where Simple bumps it: at inlining, plus once per phase boundary. Ordinary construction and
;; peepholes keep cached depths consistent along every chain, so they do not bump (see the
;; control-version section of aot.node.node for the argument).
;;
;; ## The trait boundary
;;
;; Java gets CFG-ness from subclassing, so `instanceof CFGNode` is free. Coil has no such relation
;; between `(dyn NodeOps)` and `(dyn CFGOps)`, so `cfg-of` — defined in `aot.node.control`, which is
;; where the concrete structs live — is the ONE place that knows which opcodes are control. Nothing
;; else tests op tags for control-ness: a new control opcode that only some sites learn about would
;; be treated as a data node, and GCM would try to place it.
(module aot.node.cfg)
(import "aot.util.panic" :use *)
(import "aot.node.node" :use *)

;; Simple's `CFGNode` abstract class, as a trait. A control node implements BOTH `NodeOps` and this.
(deftrait CFGOps [Self]
  ;; Back to the ordinary node view.
  (cfg-node [(self (ptr Self))] (-> (dyn NodeOps)))

  ;; Start, Region, Loop, CProj — but NOT If, Return, Stop. GCM starts a block at each of these and
  ;; the encoder emits a label for each.
  (block-head? [(self (ptr Self))] (-> bool))

  ;; The immediate dominator, or None for Start. Most kinds answer `in(0)`; Region answers the LCA
  ;; of its live inputs; Loop answers its entry.
  (cfg-idom [(self (ptr Self))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps))))

  ;; Dominator-tree depth. Most kinds answer `1 + idom.idepth`; Region answers the MAX over inputs;
  ;; Start answers 0.
  (cfg-idepth [(self (ptr Self))] (-> i64)))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Generic dominator algorithms
;;
;; The generic dominator walks (`idom-lca`) live in `aot.node.control` alongside `cfg-of`, not here.
;; They need to turn a `(dyn NodeOps)` back into a `(dyn CFGOps)`, which only the module holding the
;; concrete structs can do — and routing that through a function-pointer hook would add indirection
;; for no gain. This module is the INTERFACE; `aot.node.control` is the implementation.
